  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:u2MJzEuI0vI",
    "purpose": "Shows how to make the same five-ingredient egg fried rice on three setups: a high-powered outdoor wok burner, an indoor gas range with a wok, and a non-stick skillet on an induction or electric cooktop.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Good egg fried rice can be made on any equipment by adapting heat management and pan choice, but only high wok heat gives full wok hei smokiness.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "All three versions use the same five ingredients: dry cooked rice, three beaten eggs, three scallions, oil and light soy sauce.",
      "Rice must be dry: use day-old rice chilled uncovered, or spread fresh rice on a sheet tray to steam dry.",
      "Outdoor burner: preheat a round-bottom wok until smoking, cut the heat while cooking the eggs, then bring it back to high for the rice.",
      "Indoor gas: use a flat-bottom carbon steel wok and keep the heat on high the whole time. A butane torch can add some smokiness.",
      "Electric or induction: use a flat-bottomed non-stick, cast iron or carbon steel pan, preheat the oil to 375–400°F, and expect little or no wok hei.",
      "Cook the eggs first, set them on top of the rice and break them in. Add soy sauce around the hot edge of the pan so it sizzles.",
      "He recommends carbon steel woks about 1.5–2 mm thick and does not rinse his rice. He cooks sushi rice at about 1.1 parts water to 1 part rice."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Introduction and ingredients",
        "summary": "Kenji outlines the three methods and the five shared ingredients, and explains how to get the rice dry enough.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Outdoor wok burner method",
        "summary": "Fried rice in a 14-inch round-bottom wok on a high-output outdoor burner: smoking preheat, heat cut for the eggs, then high heat for the rice. Soy sauce goes around the edge for wok hei.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Indoor gas burner method",
        "summary": "Uses a 14-inch flat-bottom carbon steel wok on a home gas range, with advice on choosing a wok. The heat stays on high throughout, and a butane torch can add some smokiness.",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "last_segment": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "Non-stick skillet method",
        "summary": "Cooks the dish in a non-stick skillet on an induction burner, with notes on oil temperature and utensils. Also covers rice choice, water ratios, not rinsing, and alternative seasonings.",
        "first_segment": 8,
        "last_segment": 13
      }
    ],
